<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Tróndur í Gøtu</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 945–c. 1035</span> · <a href="/centuries/11th-century">11th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Tróndur í Gøtu was a Faroese chieftain known from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/faereyinga-saga">Færeyinga saga</a>, which describes him as a cunning pagan leader based at Gøta on Eysturoy. He opposed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sigmundur-brestisson">Sigmundur Brestisson</a>, who around 999 brought <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christianity">Christianity</a> to the islands for King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/olaf-tryggvason">Olaf Tryggvason</a>, and accepted baptism only under compulsion. The saga makes him responsible for Sigmundur's death, and he dominated Faroese affairs until his own death.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/trondur-i-gotu.jpg" alt="Tróndur í Gøtu" width="244" height="360" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Postverk Føroya, Graphical artist: Anker Eli Petersen · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Faroe_stamp_360_arrival_of_christianity.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
